hen you're  in the shade you have indirect exposure  then there's indirect or diffuse uv  exposure this is where the sun's uv  bounces off something else onto you  usually that something else is air  molecules high up in the sky but  sometimes it's also the ground or other  objects around you this bouncing around  also happens with visible light which is  why you can still see things when  they're in the shade shorter wavelengths  of light get diffused more so there's  more diffuse uv around the visible light  and more diffused uvb than uva if you're  standing in the middle of an empty field  then out of all the uv coming in towards  you about 60 of the uv you're getting is  diffuse and 40  is direct these percentages are very  rough and they change with lots of  different factors like how high the sun  is in the sky where you are in the world  what time of year it is what wavelength  of light you're talking about but the  key thing is the uv shining directly on  you is actually less than half of your  total uv working out how much direct uv  you're getting is pretty straightforward  if the sun is shining on you then you're  getting that direct 40  working out diffuse uv is trickier we  need to work out how much stuff is  bouncing uv on us if you're outside the  main thing bouncing uv on you is  probably the sky we can use something  called sky view to work out how much uv  we're getting so if you're standing  outside in the middle of a field you can  imagine that you're surrounded by a dome  of blue sky that is 100 exposure to  diffuse uv and the amount of diffuse uv  you're getting is proportional to the  amount of sky that you can see if  there's stuff between you and the sky  then you have lower sky view and lower  diffuse uv if half of the sky is blocked  out then you're getting about half the  diffuse uv so if you're standing right  next to a building and you're standing  in the sun then you are getting that  direct 40 uv plus half of that 60  diffuse uv that gives you a total of 70  of the uv that you would get compared to  standing in the middle of a field if  you're not in the sun but half of the  sky is blocked so you're standing next  to a bun but the sun's moved and now  you're in the shadow that means you're  not getting the direct uv anymore you're  not getting that 40  but you are still getting half of that  60 you're getting 30 percent of the uv  you would be getting if there was  nothing else in the field now in some  situations the ground can also reflect  quite a lot of uv most surfaces absorb  most of the uv that comes in so this is  like grass dirt asheville apparently  americans call this bitumen but there  are some surfaces that do reflect more  so apparently in some countries there's  this thing called snow and that can  reflect up to 90 percent if you're  standing in this so called snow the snow  can actually bounce up more uv than the  sun in the sky combined sand can be 10  to 40 depending on the type of sand  concrete is around 10  so depending on the uv index even if  you're standing in the shade all of this  bouncy uv can still add up so that's why  you can still get burnt in the shade if  you're standing and you're facing the  sky or ground that can bounce up uv you  can still get a lot of uv and remember  uvb wavelengths get scattered more when  you're in the shade you usually feel  quite cool because visible light and  infrared which is 90 of the energy  coming from the sun  that's the stuff that heats you up and  it's getting blocked but that uvb can  still bounce around and get onto you  if you face away from the sun then the  uv bouncing onto your face is going to  be cut down by a lot even though you  feel around the same temperature wise so  hats and umbrellas basically portable  shade how much protection are we getting  let's start with hats  hats are usually made with thick  material that will block out almost all  the direct uv so an akubra hat which  looks like this blocks out everything  but one two thousandth of the uv so this  leaves 0.05 getting through but of  course if you have a hat made out of  thinner fabric then the uv is going to  increase you might remember my video  about upf clothing the same points apply  here so if you're looking for a hat that  blocks out more uv you're looking for a  synthetic fabric with really tight knit  basically you want really small holes  and as few holes as possible if you have  a few layers then that helps basically  the less breathable it is the better it  is intense colors are also better  because fabric dyes can absorb uv the  bigger the hat the more sky it's going  to block out and if the brim turns down  it's going to be a bit more protective  than if the brim turns up because you  can see more sky off the side so for  example this hat  is probably going to give better  protection than  this hat  even though they are around the same  size  but they do give you hat hair this is a  terrible hat for sun protection i don't  know why i bought it  i do because i was pressured to by space  nerds  if you're wearing a cap like this you  can imagine a little man standing on  your face  he's gonna still get a lot of sky view  it will be blocked over here but you're  still getting all of this plus you're  getting some direct uv every once in a  while as well shining on your face  but a little man standing here in the  middle of your head is going to get a  lot less guy view he can't see all of  the sky up here but even with a broader  brimmed hat there can still be a really  big difference so a little man standing  here near your forehead is going to get  a lot of sky view blocked out by this  bit  like that  hats don't work with fringes  so in general the forehead and the nose  get the least sun exposure the cheeks  and the chin get the most because  they're further away from the brim  they're not getting their view blocked  out by this brim as much as if they were  up here and for parts of your face that  are angled up with the hat is going to  block more a little dude lying on your  nose is going to get more stuff blocked  out by a hat than someone lying on your  face and looking sideways but of course  there's also the ground a hat isn't  going to protect you against uv bouncing  off the ground which could be a  significant problem if you're somewhere  like in the snow one study estimated  that with the sakura a farm worker in  queensland would drop down their yearly  exposure of uv down to one-seventh for  their thyroid one-third for their nose  but by less than a half for their cheek  their overall face protection factor was  two to six but in some parts of their  face it could be up to 100  now with umbrellas we can think of these  as like hats but further away from your  head the further away it is the bigger  it has to be to block out the same  amount of sky if the umbrella doubles in  distance from your nose so  maybe to here  then the radius has to double to give  the same amount of protection  one big difference between hats and  umbrellas is that umbrellas tend to be  made out of thinner materials so usually  more direct uv can get through and this  can be really variable in studies it's  been found that regular rain umbrellas  can let through as much as 23 of the uv  or as little as 0.1 percent  unsurprisingly given what we know about  fabrics from upf clothing black  umbrellas work the best and also  umbrellas that have a upf rating this  umbrella from uniqlo claims to be upf 40  which means it lets through 2.5 percent  of uv i'm not sure i believe the upf  rating for this umbrella but it's  supposedly bigger than 50 plus so  it should be letting in less than two  percent another difference with  umbrellas is you're usually off to the  side with an umbrella so one side of  your face can get a lot more uv and  depending on where your umbrella is and  how wide it is you could actually be  getting more uv exposure with your  umbrella than with a hat but on the  bright side you don't get hat hair  annoyingly for our purposes most studies  have been done on beach umbrellas which  are stuck in the ground and much bigger  overall in studies with beach umbrellas  you can get a projection factor of  between 3 and 17 depending on the size  of the umbrella where you are under the  umbrella the thickness of the umbrella  where the sun is and so on another study  estimated that if you're sitting in the  shade under an umbrella in the middle of  summer and you're not wearing any other  protection as illustrated by these  helpful but slightly creepy diagrams you  can still get burned in two hours so  here are the takeaways hats and  umbrellas are great for giving uv  protection that you don't need to  reapply and won't wear off quickly they  also project areas that you might not  easily be able to cover with sunscreen  or clothing like your scalp or like the  edge of your eyelids although there are  sunglasses for that that will be a  different video they're particularly  good for protecting against direct uv  but it's that 60 diffuse uv that gets  you these umbrellas and hats only really  protect the circle of sky right above  your head there's all of the edge where  stuff can bounce in to project against  diffuse uv you're going to need sideways  protection like a wall or trees to get  between you and the sky or you can go  for something that's closer to your skin  like sunscreens and clothing if you're  in a high sun exposure situation  remember the swiss cheese layers don't  just rely on one type of protection use  multiple types high sun exposure  includes going to the beach and  exercising in the sun in lower uv  exposure situations like if you're only  going outside for a few minutes or if  it's in winter unless you're in one of  those places that gets this snow thing  then you're going to need less layers of  swiss cheese so i hope this video was  useful you might also like my videos  about whether or not you need to wear  sunscreen indoors whether or not you  need to reapply sunscreen how to reapply  sunscreen my video on sun protective  clothing and maybe just my sunscreen  videos in general like subscribe  notification bell check out my blog  check me out on instagram and i will see  you next time for more nerdery

